  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Is the function $f(x,y,z)=2xyz-{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}z$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{n}f(x,y,z).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty,tz) & =2(tx)(ty)(tz)-(ty)^{3}+(ty)^{2}(tz) \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}xyz-{t}^{3}{y}^{3}+{t}^{3}{y}^{2}z \\\\  & ={t}^{3}(2xyz-{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}z) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}f(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{3}f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y,z)$ is $3.$",
